  "answer_markdown": "For an initial evaluation, I conditionally prefer [DLRover](https://github.com/intelligent-machine-learning/dlrover) if the modernization task concerns fault tolerance and scaling of distributed training. The supplied host README read dated 2026-10-11 confirms these stated capabilities and clarifies the Kubernetes/PyTorch path: a wrapper around scripts runnable through `torchrun` or `torch.distributed.run`; the Ray architecture is described separately. This strengthens the preference for that scenario, but compatibility and operability on your platform have not been verified. The catalog remains a snapshot of observations dated 2026-09-01; the host read does not update it. DLRover's license is recorded as `Other` in the snapshot; its terms and acceptability are unknown.\n\nFor component comparison, [veScale](https://github.com/volcengine/veScale) is presented in the snapshot as a tool for distributed LLM/RL training on PyTorch, and [NVIDIA-NeMo/Automodel](https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Automodel) as an LLM/VLM training library on PyTorch Distributed with stated Hugging Face support. Their fit for the task remains unknown: no separate host reads are supplied for them. These are training-layer options, and a combined stack with DLRover is not yet justified. The proposed arrangement for validation is: retained training script → launch adapter → selected component; the existence and structure of the current path are unknown. Data, checkpoints, the job queue, integration, and operational rules remain the team's responsibility; their ownership and component access should be defined before connection.\n\nFor an equivalent comparison scope—running one target job with agreed recovery requirements—reuse requires adaptation, dependency verification, and maintenance of the selected version; custom development requires implementing and maintaining the same mechanisms, while the current or native path may already cover the requirements. Savings have not been established. Workload type, language and platform, hardware, versions, license, and recovery requirements can change the decision. The single planning scenario is a hypothetical 4–8 engineer-hours for first validation, with low confidence: one engineer familiar with the target application and component interface, at 8 hours per engineer-day. Before integration, the workload, current path, language/platform, selected version, and license must be confirmed; validation is limited to source qualification or an isolated synthetic fixture, without installation, credentials, a paid provider, or production data. Full adoption effort and elapsed time are unknown.\n\nThe proposed first validation is to compare one job's interface with the documented launch method for the selected version and record dependencies, license terms, and criteria for subsequent recovery validation; the outcome will be a bounded handoff of integration requirements. The preference for DLRover would change if the task primarily concerns the training layer, its path is incompatible with the platform, or the current mechanism already satisfies the requirements. The trial should remain isolated, without access to authoritative data or the queue: abandoning it preserves them without migration or loss; a concrete return to the existing path cannot yet be described because that path is unknown. All steps have the status `proposed_not_executed`; implementation requires a separate explicit request. Which gap needs to be closed first: fault tolerance and launch scaling, or distributing computation within training?",
